7: 7.1 Hidden-Lattice Decision Bounded Distance Decoding\nL2830@P37: We first define the hidden lattice BDD (HLBDD) distribution, and then the decisional problem\nL2831@P37: associated with it. We use Gaussian noise but other noise distributions can be considered as well,\nL2832@P37: the property that we use in our proof is that the distribution is bounded.\nL2833@P37: Definition 7.3 (Hidden Lattice BDD Distribution). Let L1 and L2 be two given lattices, and let\nL2834@P37: L be a finite family of lattices, where each member L\nL2835@P37: 0 ∈ L satisfies L1 ⊆ L0 ⊆ L2. Let r ∈ G be a\nL2836@P37: Gaussian parameter. The Hidden Lattice BDD Distribution over L2/L1, denoted by CL1,L,DL2,r, is\nL2837@P37: sampled by choosing uniformly at random a lattice L\nL2838@P37: 0\nL2839@P37: $\nL2840@P37: ← L, an element x\nL2841@P37: $← L0/L1, and an error\nL2842@P37: term e←DL2,r and outputting y = x + e mod L1.\nL2843@P37: One should think of the lattice L2 as the “ambient space”, i.e. Z\nL2844@P37: n\nL2845@P37: for general Euclidean setting\nL2846@P37: or the ring of integer R in the algebraic setting. Note that it is possible to define the distribution\nL2847@P37: with a continuous noise term e. The usual connection between discrete and continuous distribution\nL2848@P37: from LWE / RLWE apply here as well (see, e.g., [LPR13, Lemma 2.23]).\nL2849@P37: Definition 7.4 (HLBDD Problem). Let L1,L2, L, r be as in Definition 7.3. The HLBDD Problem,\nL2850@P37: denote by HLBDDL1,L,DL2,r\nL2851@P37: is to distinguish between two samples from the distribution CL1,L,DL2,r\nL2852@P37: ,\nL2853@P37: and two samples from the uniform distribution over L2/L.\nL2854@P37: For the purpose of this section we will set L2 = R (the ring of integers of our number field) and\nL2855@P37: L = Pk, for k = n\nL2856@P37: Ω(1)\nL2857@P37: .\nL2858@P37: Hardness and Variants. We defined HLBDD as the problem where the distinguisher only gets\nL2859@P37: 2 samples from the HLBDD distribution. This is the mini